/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Every cell is wrapped in a lipid membrane.
   - At rest, K⁺ leak channels are more open than Na⁺ leak channels.
   - Vm is membrane voltage; Vinside is the voltage inside the cell; Voutside is the voltage outside.
   - For one ion, the Nernst equation gives the equilibrium voltage: the membrane voltage at which that ion's concentration push is exactly balanced by the electric pull, so it stops moving on balance.
   - Eion is the equilibrium (Nernst) voltage, inside with respect to outside, in mV; z is the ion's charge (+1 for Na⁺ and K⁺, −1 for Cl⁻); [ion]out and [ion]in are the concentrations outside and inside in mmol/L. 61.5 mV is 2.303 RT/F at body 
   - The Goldman equation combines the ions, each weighted by how easily it crosses the membrane (its permeability P).
   - PK, PNa and PCl are the relative permeabilities (only their ratio matters).
   - An action potential is a rapid change in Vm that travels along a nerve or muscle fibre.
   - Na⁺ enters, so Vm rises quickly toward positive values.
   - After a spike the membrane has a refractory period.
*/
