/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Nerve and muscle currents are carried by ions, mainly Na⁺, K⁺ and Cl⁻, in salt water.
   - The reaction leaves electrons on the metal and a sheet of ions in the liquid just outside it.
   - A recording uses two electrodes, each with its own half-cell potential.
   - When current flows, the half-cell potential shifts from equilibrium.
   - Model one electrode as a DC source Ehc in series with a resistance Rs, shunted by a capacitance Cd and a leakage resistance Rd.
   - Vbio is the bio-potential generated by the tissue in volts; Ze is the electrode impedance at that frequency in ohms; Zin is the amplifier input impedance in ohms; Vin is the voltage actually present at the amplifier terminals.
   - To keep the loss under 1%, Zin must be at least 100 times Ze.
   - Impedance is frequency dependent, so a mismatch shows up differently in the ECG and EEG bands.
   - Surface electrodes sit on the skin and record the summed activity of many cells below.
   - Disposable costs more per electrode but saves setup time and gives repeatable impedance.
*/
