/* book exam traps: Do not treat the electrode offset as real bio-potential. It is a DC half-cell potential and must be blocked by the amplifier or cancelled by matched electrodes. | A good electrode does not have zero half-cell potential. Matching the two electrodes is what cancels it; a mismatched pair leaves a standing DC that can saturate the amplifier. | High electrode impedance also raises thermal noise and, if the two electrodes differ, converts common-mode mains into a differential voltage, so CMRR falls. | Microelectrode tip resistance is the pipette's own series resistance, not the cell membrane resistance. Candidates confuse the two in numerical problems. | Unipolar and bipolar describe the arrangement, not the shape. The same disc is unipolar in one lead and part of a bipolar pair in another; read which one the question describes. */
