/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Selection criteria.
   - The main families differ in what changes.
   - A strain gauge is a thin metal or semiconductor foil bonded to a spring element.
   - GF is the gauge factor, dimensionless; ΔR is the change in resistance in Ω; R is the unstrained resistance in Ω; ε is strain, the fractional change in length, dimensionless.
   - Metal foil gauges have GF near 2; semiconductor gauges are higher but more temperature-sensitive.
   - ΔV is the bridge output in V for a quarter bridge; Vex is the excitation voltage in V; GF is the gauge factor; ε is strain.
   - A half bridge gives twice this output, a full bridge four times.
*/
